column5:
After the product-owner demo, the feedback was that Scout needs more styling. A design review produced a polish pass that keeps Scout's structure and our design system. This ticket applies it.

*Scope*

* Type, spacing, radius and states (hover, focus, disabled, pressed) for the Scout shell, the history list, the thread, chat bubbles, message actions, hint and Done chips, result cards, question items, the approval card, teaching set and search results, What Scout can do, the composer, evidence, loading and error states, and the tour.
* Load Poppins 600 and 700. Scout uses those weights, but only 100–500 are loaded today.
* Small layout changes:
** bold lead-ins become sub-heads;
** card notes move into the card header;
** the approval card gets one note paragraph and a tinted header;
** result meta lines merge into one;
** evidence rows put the name and Open work on one line;
** on phones, filter chips scroll in one row, and the history menu button shows on hover or focus;
** student-name lists show in two columns when wide.

*Unchanged:* what Scout does, our colour tokens and primary colour, the fonts and icons, the Scout mark, the top bar, menu, search and hamburger, and the Proceed and Not now labels.

*Acceptance*

* Each screen matches its "after" design.
* Text meets WCAG AA contrast, with visible focus states.
* It works at phone width (390 px).
* Nothing a teacher can do changes.

column7:
14/Oct/26 2:59 AM;62850ed5bdf2f30067d3f477;[~accountid:712020:feb1342f-9201-444f-b941-ea0c318640ae] 

{quote}# Grading in What Scout Can Do: this is by design. What Scout Can Do doesn’t list grading; grading is offered inside Grade Now, where Scout’s suggestions include “Make a rubric for this question” and “Show a strong and a weak answer” on a written question. Asking Scout in your own words still works from anywhere. The search box’s example used to say “rubric”, which found nothing there, so it now suggests “quiz” and “reading level”, which do.
# Going through the questions: fixed on dev2. When Scout moves Grade Now to another question, it now gets that question and the student’s answer back, so it reviews that answer straight away instead of asking you to paste it.{quote}

column5:
The sales pilot's District Co-Pilot answers leadership questions and drafts board and principal reports. Districts are the likely buyers of Scout (paid per school or district), and today Scout offers them nothing. This ticket produces the plan for an administrator Scout, not the feature. We define the features ourselves, with no customer input; customers see the finished feature and give feedback after it ships. Building it needs the "Scout is teacher-only" rule lifted for this work.

*Acceptance criteria*

# A phase-1 question list defined by us from the pilot's prompts ("Which schools need support?", "What PD should we prioritize?", "Compare implementation and learning", "What should principals know?") and from what our district and school reports can answer, each question mapped to the report and fields that answer it, or marked as not answerable from our data.
# Data completeness measured across all districts on the dev snapshot and published with ids and denominators: share of classes with any graded assessment in the last 8 weeks, usage per school, standards coverage. It states whether "which schools need support" can be told apart from "which schools grade less", and drops or rewords questions the data can't support.
# A design note covering:
#* which existing reports feed it (district and school usage reports, district and school assessment scores, standards);
#* figures computed by our code and traceable to a report, with Scout only explaining and drafting;
#* school and class totals only, no student-level data sent to the model;
#* scoping: principals see only their school; district admins with a district but no school set; users with several roles; masquerade;
#* the exportable "district summary" and "principal brief";
#* how answers are checked before release.
# A build ticket for phase 1, with an estimate, proposed for approval.

*Not included:* any code for the administrator Scout; customer interviews.

*One ticket for the whole administrator Scout.* Everything is built, tested and QA'd here; there is no phase 2 and no separate plan, build or follow-up tickets. QA feedback goes on this ticket. The plan behind it is ONLINE-7798 (done).

*Scope.* For district admins and school admins whose district has the flag on ({{FEATURE_FRONTEND_AI_ADMIN_ASSISTANT}}, requiring the Scout flag):

(a) a server-side figure layer for digital activity, grading practice, assessment results and standards results per district, school and class, each figure with as-of, population, formula and the report it reproduces;  
(b) Scout answering the 14 questions of the plan's list from those figures only, with a deterministic coverage/confidence answer, drafts (email to principals) and no actions;  
(c) two exports built from the same figures: district summary and principal brief (PDF and CSV);  
(d) a pulse page for district and school admins;  
(e) classroom-practice evidence from walkthroughs (what the plan called phase 2): what print use and classroom practice the data supports, added to the digest and the questions it can answer, with the same provenance rules;  
(f) scoping by caller (district, school, several roles, masquerade read-only with exports off, homeschool/demo excluded), audit log, flag.

*Decided (Salman):* teacher names shown by default in the app and always in exports; district admin sees everything in the district, school admin everything in the school, teacher their own students (the model still gets only school, class and teacher totals, never student rows); named teacher lists one school at a time; staff off; wording "digital activity", never "implementation"; questions the data cannot support are dropped or reworded, with the coverage sentence where a figure exists for only some schools.

*Out of scope.* The "Your Studies Weekly partner" card; AI usage/cost page; pacing against a calendar; any write action; student-level data; changing the existing reports; the district-admin cross-district read gaps (H10, separate security ticket).

*Acceptance.* Every number in a Scout reply or export exists in the turn's digest (provenance screen, zero violations on the eval set); no student identifier anywhere in a digest, reply or export (pin); 403/404 matrix passes for wrong role, other district, other school, flag off, homeschool, demo, NULL district; figures equal the existing reports' where both exist, with the named differences listed in the MR; teachers unchanged (credits response byte-identical; teacher prompt/registry/layout checks green); admin eval gate recorded before the run, control and arm in the same run.

*Estimate.* 9 SP covers parts a-d and f (figure layer and scoping 3, admin Scout 3, exports and pulse page 2, verification 1). The walkthrough evidence (e) was not in that estimate: size it first thing in the work and update the story points here.

*Known data limits (dev2, 2026-10-11).* dev2 has no usage data, so per-school usage and the needs-support vs grades-less comparison are unmeasured ("needs production data"); the all-district figure queries take 17-27 minutes each, so the figure layer needs a per-district or rollup read, not an all-district compute per request.

